Title: "Rational Numbers"
Id: "8043e76c-b740-4ec1-aded-14630e21d209"


Text: "While the integers $\mathbb{Z}$ resolve the asymmetry of subtraction in
       the natural numbers, they remain closed under division only in
       exceptional circumstances: equations of the form $b \cdot x = a$ with
       $b \neq 0$ admit solutions in $\mathbb{Z}$ if and only if $b$ divides
       $a$. To endow our arithmetic universe with complete division by nonzero
       quantities, we must construct the field of fractions $\mathbb{Q}$.

       Geometrically and physically, rational numbers correspond to ratios of
       commensurable magnitudes—fractions of an indivisible unit measure.
       Algebraically, they represent the smallest field containing the integral
       domain $\mathbb{Z}$. Abstractly, $\mathbb{Q}$ forms an ordered field in
       which addition and multiplication interact via distributivity, every
       nonzero element possesses a unique multiplicative inverse, and the order
       $\leq$ respects translation and scaling by positive elements. Concretely,
       a rational number is represented by a pair
       $(a, b) \in \mathbb{Z} \times (\mathbb{Z} \setminus \{0\})$, interpreted
       as $a/b$, where two formal quotients $a/b$ and $c/d$ designate the same
       rational quantity precisely when their cross-products coincide:
       $ad = bc$."
Id: "4a68d496-756c-499c-8be9-dbcf0fd13074"


SectionTitle: "The Abstract Rational Field"
Id: "b792d225-7464-47d1-98fb-4d8003f47838"


[\rationals]
Defines: Qb ::= (Q, 0, 1, +, *, neg(q_), inv(q_), leq(a_, b_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. Q is \set
. 0 "in" Q
. 1 "in" Q
. + is \binary.operation:on{Q}
. * is \binary.operation:on{Q}
. neg is \function:on{Q}:to{Q}
. inv is \function:on{Q}:to{Q}
. leq is \total.order:on{Q}
expresses:
. 0 != 1
. inv(0) = 0
. forAll: a, b, c "in" Q
  then:
  . (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)
  . a + b = b + a
  . a + 0 = a
  . a + neg(a) = 0
  . (a * b) * c = a * (b * c)
  . a * b = b * a
  . a * 1 = a
  . a * (b + c) = (a * b) + (a * c)
  . if: a != 0
    then: a * inv(a) = 1
  . if: leq(a, b)
    then: leq(a + c, b + c)
. forAll: a, b "in" Q
  where:
  . leq(0, a)
  . leq(0, b)
  then: leq(0, a * b)
Enables:
. capability: q_ "in" Qb :-> q_ is \rational
. view:
  as: S := Q is \set
Documented:
. called: "the rationals"
. written: "\mathbb{Q}"
. description: "The rational numbers as an abstract ordered-field tuple
                $(Q,0,1,+,\cdot,-,(\cdot)^{-1},\leq)$."
Id: "8454e27c-2e90-4944-a36b-27b66be5f4cf"


[\rational]
Declares: q "in" \rationals
Enables:
. capability: a_ + b_ :=> a_ \.rational.+./ b_
. capability: a_ * b_ :=> a_ \.rational.*./ b_
. capability: -a_ :=> \rational.neg(a_)
. capability: a_ <= b_ :=> a_ \:rational.leq:/ b_
Documented:
. called: "rational"
. description: "A rational number is a member of the underlying carrier
                $\mathbb{Q}$."
Id: "2250d77e-8840-464c-be09-6a1768db38bc"


[\rational.zero]
Defines: z := \rationals..0 is \rational
Documented:
. called: "rational zero"
. written: "0"
Id: "129d3bee-fc92-48a9-8823-3f9a290a3540"


[\rational.one]
Defines: o := \rationals..1 is \rational
Documented:
. called: "rational one"
. written: "1"
Id: "d561070d-99fb-41e0-966e-ede168509eb2"


[a_ \.rational.+./ b_]
Defines: a_ + b_ is \binary.operation:on{\rationals}
expresses: a_ + b_ := \rationals..`+`(a_, b_)
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ plus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? + b_?"
Id: "44f276f5-5ec9-4d24-a891-ca3c18cba5b6"


[a_ \.rational.*./ b_]
Defines: a_ * b_ is \binary.operation:on{\rationals}
expresses: a_ * b_ := \rationals..`*`(a_, b_)
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ times $b_?$"
. written: "a_? \cdot b_?"
Id: "a63fb60f-01bb-49d3-815e-a85f721c9ac0"


[\rational.neg(a_)]
Defines: n := \rationals..neg(a_) is \rational
Documented:
. called: "negative of $a_?$"
. written: "-a_?"
Id: "b1356b4a-3178-43e1-8013-b993ba8b2c36"


[\rational.inv(a_)]
Defines: i := \rationals..inv(a_) is \rational
Documented:
. called: "reciprocal of $a_?$"
. written: "a_?^{-1}"
Id: "b567f507-0351-483f-9833-95109ab73409"


[a_ \.rational.-./ b_]
Defines: d := a_ \.rational.+./ \rational.neg(b_) is \rational
when: a_, b_ is \rational
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ minus $b_?$"
. written: "a_? - b_?"
Id: "03fffc82-39e3-4aba-b4e1-6cb950895674"


[a_ \.rational.div./ b_]
Defines: d := a_ \.rational.*./ \rational.inv(b_) is \rational
when: a_, b_ is \rational
Documented:
. called: "$a_?$ divided by $b_?$"
. written: "\frac{a_?}{b_?}"
Id: "02b2f688-232d-4451-aa55-d17214af0bf0"


[a \:rational.leq:/ b]
Declares: a is \rational
when: b is \rational
satisfies: \rationals..leq(a, b)
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than or equal to $b?$"
. written: "a? \leq b?"
Id: "f7389fcf-20d8-4465-8bfa-ec1fbe033c51"


[a \:rational.less:/ b]
Declares: a is \rational
when: b is \rational
satisfies:
. a \:rational.leq?:/ b
. a != b
Documented:
. called: "$a?$ is less than $b?$"
. written: "a? < b?"
Id: "5a472809-b0d1-46c0-9a80-236ea2ab148f"


[\rational.abs(q_)]
Defines: abs(q_) is \function:on{\rationals}:to{\rationals}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: \rational.zero \:rational.leq?:/ q_
  then: abs(q_) := q_
  else: abs(q_) := \rational.neg(q_)
Documented:
. called: "absolute value of $q_?$"
. written: "\left|q_?\right|"
Id: "61cb32b3-cb61-43ac-8319-8cf7315fe9ab"


SectionTitle: "Realization by Fractions of Integers"
Id: "a34389fb-655b-4573-a3ab-85221cbea87c"


[\nonzero.integer]
Declares: z is \integer
satisfies: z != \integer.zero
Documented:
. called: "nonzero integer"
. description: "An integer distinct from zero."
Id: "087662f6-7cca-4ecb-b7df-883cb10c6044"


[\nonzero.integers]
Defines: Zstar := \set@{z_ : z_ is \nonzero.integer} is \set
Documented:
. called: "nonzero integers"
. written: "\mathbb{Z} \setminus \{0\}"
Id: "7632dfa6-b8a4-49a3-ae26-6efff1be4e24"


[\rational.pair.base]
Defines: P := \set@{(a_, b_) : a_ is \integer; b_ is \nonzero.integer} is \set
Documented:
. called: "rational fraction pairs"
. written: "\mathbb{Z} \times (\mathbb{Z} \setminus \{0\})"
Id: "f7174275-17fc-4a81-a133-96bf1a5b956e"


[\rationals.relation]
Defines: R is \equivalence.relation:on{\rational.pair.base}
expresses:
. forAll:
  . a, c is \integer
  . b, d is \nonzero.integer
  then:
  . have: R((a, b), (c, d))
    iff: a \.integer.*./ d = c \.integer.*./ b
Documented:
. called: "rational equivalence relation"
. description: "The relation $(a,b) \sim (c,d)$ exactly when $ad=cb$."
Id: "24804175-428b-44db-8573-0165be44818c"


[\rational.pair.carrier]
Defines: Q := \rational.pair.base \.mod./ \rationals.relation is \set
Documented:
. called: "rational pair quotient"
. written: "(\mathbb{Z} \times (\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}))/\sim"
Id: "cb5afd49-aedb-4cf5-8832-249a80e90743"


[\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}]
Defines: C := \equivalence.class:of{(a, b)}:over{\rationals.relation}#using{X := \rational.pair.base} "in" \rational.pair.carrier
when:
. a is \integer
. b is \nonzero.integer
Documented:
. called: "rational class of $a?$ over $b?$"
. written: "[(a?,b?)]_{\mathbb{Q}}"
Id: "a5808b94-fc5f-459d-8a3b-ab72b6650e80"


[\rational.pair.denominator.one]
Defines: d is \nonzero.integer
expresses: d = \integer.one
Documented:
. called: "unit denominator"
. written: "1"
Id: "2b3d1b0d-1375-4a6b-8670-397c65be411d"


[\rational.pair.zero]
Defines: z := \rational.pair.class:of{\integer.zero, \rational.pair.denominator.one} "in" \rational.pair.carrier
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair zero"
. written: "[(0,1)]_{\mathbb{Q}}"
Id: "c3eea23f-dd4a-457e-8b79-c66e804059c1"


[\rational.pair.one]
Defines: o := \rational.pair.class:of{\integer.one, \rational.pair.denominator.one} "in" \rational.pair.carrier
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair one"
. written: "[(1,1)]_{\mathbb{Q}}"
Id: "0286b427-e7ba-4c7b-baab-2e7f83324e52"


[\rational.pair.add]
Defines: add(x_, y_) is \binary.operation:on{\rational.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll:
  . a, c is \integer
  . b, d is \nonzero.integer
  then:
  . let:
    . n is \integer
    . e is \nonzero.integer
    where:
    . n = (a \.integer.*./ d) \.integer.+./ (c \.integer.*./ b)
    . e = b \.integer.*./ d
    then: add(\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}, \rational.pair.class:of{c, d}) = \rational.pair.class:of{n, e}
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair addition"
. description: "$[(a,b)]+[(c,d)]=[(ad+cb,bd)]$."
Id: "bb76baf9-98f2-4fae-9be3-7dd96eff0d8b"


[\rational.pair.mul]
Defines: mul(x_, y_) is \binary.operation:on{\rational.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll:
  . a, c is \integer
  . b, d is \nonzero.integer
  then:
  . let:
    . n is \integer
    . e is \nonzero.integer
    where:
    . n = a \.integer.*./ c
    . e = b \.integer.*./ d
    then: mul(\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}, \rational.pair.class:of{c, d}) = \rational.pair.class:of{n, e}
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair multiplication"
. description: "$[(a,b)]\cdot[(c,d)]=[(ac,bd)]$."
Id: "763fb9de-5327-499f-a3c9-aed218accf56"


[\rational.pair.neg(q_)]
Defines: neg(q_) is \function:on{\rational.pair.carrier}:to{\rational.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll:
  . a is \integer
  . b is \nonzero.integer
  then: neg(\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}) = \rational.pair.class:of{\integer.neg(a), b}
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair negation"
. description: "$-[(a,b)]=[(-a,b)]$."
Id: "31f2155e-d1ab-4a4c-b3a8-a319c5bc7097"


[\rational.pair.inv(q_)]
Defines: inv(q_) is \function:on{\rational.pair.carrier}:to{\rational.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. inv(\rational.pair.zero) = \rational.pair.zero
. forAll:
  . a is \nonzero.integer
  . b is \nonzero.integer
  then: inv(\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}) = \rational.pair.class:of{b, a}
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair reciprocal"
. description: "Reciprocal swaps numerator and denominator for nonzero classes,
                with zero sent to zero."
Id: "196a8359-eaf0-4536-91c7-319787d07098"


[\rational.pair.leq]
Defines: leq(x_, y_) is \total.order:on{\rational.pair.carrier}
expresses:
. forAll:
  . a, c is \integer
  . b, d is \nonzero.integer
  then:
  . let: ad, cb, bd is \integer
    where:
    . ad = a \.integer.*./ d
    . cb = c \.integer.*./ b
    . bd = b \.integer.*./ d
    then:
    . have: leq(\rational.pair.class:of{a, b}, \rational.pair.class:of{c, d})
      iff:
      . anyOf:
        . allOf:
          . \integer.zero \:integer.leq?:/ bd
          . ad \:integer.leq?:/ cb
        . allOf:
          . bd \:integer.leq?:/ \integer.zero
          . cb \:integer.leq?:/ ad
Documented:
. called: "fraction-pair order"
. description: "Cross-multiplication defines the order, with the comparison
                reversed when the denominator product is negative."
Id: "29f4443f-9ea6-4f02-a62d-b278fbc4501a"


[\rational.pair.realization]
Realizes: Qb ::= (Q, 0, 1, +, *, neg(q_), inv(q_), leq(a_, b_)) := \rationals
specifies:
. Q := \rational.pair.carrier
. 0 := \rational.pair.zero
. 1 := \rational.pair.one
. + := \rational.pair.add
. * := \rational.pair.mul
. neg := \rational.pair.neg
. inv := \rational.pair.inv
. leq := \rational.pair.leq
Enables:
. capability: q_ "in" Qb :<->: q_ "in" Q
Documented:
. called: "rationals as fractions of integers"
. description: "The concrete realization of the abstract rational tuple by
                equivalence classes of nonzero-denominator integer pairs."
Id: "6ef88cb1-eef6-4ced-8f2c-a4fdacaeea4e"


SectionTitle: "Field of Fractions and Density"
Id: "4c03c412-c158-493b-835f-5ffdaa1769a3"


Text: "The formal fraction-pair construction realizes a rational number as an
       equivalence class $[(a, b)]_{\mathbb{Q}}$ of integer pairs $(a, b) \in \mathbb{Z} \times (\mathbb{Z} \setminus \{0\})$.
       The familiar fraction notation $a/b$ designates this entire equivalence class
       rather than a specific pair of integers.
       
       A critical obligation of this quotient construction is verifying
       *well-definedness*: if $(a, b) \sim (a', b')$ and $(c, d) \sim (c', d')$,
       then the defined addition and multiplication must produce equivalent outputs:
       $$ (ad + cb, bd) \sim (a'd' + c'b', b'd') \quad \text{and} \quad (ac, bd) \sim (a'c', b'd'). $$
       Similarly, the ordering condition must be independent of chosen representatives.
       The map $\iota : \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}$ defined by $\iota(n) = [(n, 1)]_{\mathbb{Q}}$
       is an injective ring homomorphism, allowing us to identify $\mathbb{Z}$ canonically
       as an ordered subring of $\mathbb{Q}$."
Id: "694343ad-950a-425c-9b62-705e6bb66956"


TextTheorem: "The embedding $\iota : \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{Q}$
              identifies each integer $a$ with $a/1$. The field of fractions
              $\mathbb{Q}$ satisfies a universal property: if $F$ is any field
              and $\varphi : \mathbb{Z} \to F$ is a non-trivial ring
              homomorphism, there exists a unique field homomorphism
              $\widetilde{\varphi} : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ such that
              $\widetilde{\varphi} \circ \iota = \varphi$. Specifically,
              $\widetilde{\varphi}(a/b) = \varphi(a) \cdot \varphi(b)^{-1}$.
              Thus $\mathbb{Q}$ is the initial object in the category of fields
              of characteristic zero."
Id: "90b5985b-53fa-4b79-b315-eaae93791621"


Theorem:
given: p, q is \rational
where: p \:rational.less?:/ q
then:
. exists: r is \rational
  suchThat:
  . p \:rational.less?:/ r
  . r \:rational.less?:/ q
Documented:
. called: "Density of the Rational Order"
. description: "Between any two distinct rational numbers lies another rational
                number."
Proof: """Let $p, q \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $p < q$. We define their midpoint
          (arithmetic mean) as: $$ r = (p + q) \cdot 2^{-1} = \frac{p + q}{2} $$
          where $2 = 1 + 1 \in \mathbb{Q}$. Since $1 > 0$, we have $2 > 0$, and
          in any ordered field, the multiplicative inverse of a strictly
          positive element is strictly positive, so $2^{-1} > 0$.

          First, since $p < q$, adding $p$ to both sides gives:
          $$ p + p < p + q \implies 2 \cdot p < p + q $$ Multiplying both sides
          by $2^{-1} > 0$ preserves the strict inequality:
          $$ p = (2 \cdot p) \cdot 2^{-1} < (p + q) \cdot 2^{-1} = r $$

          Second, adding $q$ to both sides of $p < q$ gives:
          $$ p + q < q + q \implies p + q < 2 \cdot q $$ Multiplying both sides
          by $2^{-1} > 0$ yields:
          $$ r = (p + q) \cdot 2^{-1} < (2 \cdot q) \cdot 2^{-1} = q $$

          Combining these results, we obtain $p < r < q$. Since the rationals
          are closed under addition and multiplication by reciprocals,
          $r \in \mathbb{Q}$. Thus the rational order is dense: between any two
          distinct rational numbers lies an intermediate rational."""
Id: "ca6475d3-548d-402f-822b-87241cb23f3e"


Text: "Although the rational order is dense—meaning there are no 'gaps' in the
       sense of immediate predecessors or successors—$\mathbb{Q}$ nonetheless
       exhibits profound structural incompleteness.

       A classic demonstration discovered by the Pythagoreans is the
       incommensurability of the diagonal of a unit square: no rational number
       satisfies $x^2 = 2$. For if $x = a/b$ with $\gcd(a, b) = 1$, then
       $a^2 = 2b^2$ forces $a$ to be even, say $a = 2k$. Substituting gives
       $4k^2 = 2b^2$, so $b^2 = 2k^2$, forcing $b$ to be even as well,
       contradicting coprimality.

       In order-theoretic terms, the subset
       $S = \{q \in \mathbb{Q} : q \leq 0 \text{ or } q^2 < 2\}$ is bounded
       above (for example, by $2$), but has no least upper bound in
       $\mathbb{Q}$. Similarly, Cauchy sequences of rationals like the
       truncations of $\sqrt{2}$ fail to converge to a limit in $\mathbb{Q}$.
       This defect necessitates the construction of the real continuum
       $\mathbb{R}$ via Dedekind cuts or metric completions."
Id: "f9d776ab-08da-44ec-a49e-93c8c2257dad"


TextTheorem: "Every rational number has a decimal expansion that either
              terminates (has only finitely many nonzero fractional digits) or
              is eventually periodic. Conversely, every eventually periodic
              decimal expansion represents a rational number.

              This dichotomy follows from the division algorithm: when dividing
              integer $a$ by nonzero integer $b$, each step produces a remainder
              in $\{0, 1, \dots, |b|-1\}$. By the Pigeonhole Principle, after at
              most $|b|$ division steps, either a remainder of $0$ is reached
              (yielding a terminating expansion) or a previously seen remainder
              recurs, forcing all subsequent quotient digits to repeat in a
              cycle of length at most $|b|-1$. Conversely, an eventually
              periodic expansion can be summed via a geometric series into a
              closed-form ratio of integers."
Id: "ca42d107-8793-4115-9298-b49f3e8e348a"
